Draw the **Lattice lane splitter**, an 8-frame **sprite sheet** of the two-tile belt
unzipper, using only the drawing tool.

The lane splitter is a piece of the **Lattice** factory: a top-down **belt
unzipper** that spans **two tiles** across the flow — a standalone machine that takes
**a single belt input on one side and unzips it into two belt outputs on the other**,
splitting that one belt's two lanes apart and routing each lane out to the **outer
lane** of its own output. The one input sits on the **top (anchor) cell** — aligned to
that tile so a belt butts up flush — and the bottom cell's West is closed off by the
housing. The splitting happens inside, **hidden under a solid housing**; you see the
single belt feeding in on the West, the two belts leaving on the East, and the closed
housing in the middle. To show the machine at work, the housing carries a raised **splitting
ridge down its centre** aimed at the input and **two moving spreader heads that ride
outward** toward the two outputs, and its output end is drawn as an East-pointing
arrow so its feed direction is clear. You are drawing the *device* seen from above —
a component belts plug into, not a length of belt — with the belt surface at its
mouths and the two spreader heads animated frame to frame.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a
time**, each targeting a frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you
issue are recorded to a per-frame action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×64 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 0 --x 0 --y 0 --w 32 --h 64 --color "#34383d"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the lane splitter sprite sheet up operation by operation until each
`frames/<index>.png` matches the brief: one two-tile unzipper spanning both 32×32
cells, with a single belt input on the top (anchor) cell of the West edge (the bottom
cell's West closed off by the housing) and two outputs on the East, a solid grey-blue
housing hiding the mechanism, a central East-West splitting ridge, two spreader heads
that ride outward toward the outputs to convey the input's two lanes being pulled
apart, an East-pointing arrow on the housing's output end, and the amber chevrons at
the belt mouths scrolling East across the eight frames in a seamless loop. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-frame action logs are
your finished submission.
